{"authored" [{"question" "q-131014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={x}^{6}$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "2e96ba684debc6206d03e649c33b7e00c7038d419c650384ad50a25aa582aea9" "basis" 2014 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-131014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{6}={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $4x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})={x}^{2}+{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "2e96ba684debc6206d03e649c33b7e00c7038d419c650384ad50a25aa582aea9" "basis" 2014 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-131014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3393391076fea76f8c79cef9647b68f7e013529113d547524bda34e394f52acd" "basis" 2014 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-131014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "2e96ba684debc6206d03e649c33b7e00c7038d419c650384ad50a25aa582aea9" "basis" 2014 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-131014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "2e96ba684debc6206d03e649c33b7e00c7038d419c650384ad50a25aa582aea9" "basis" 2014 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-131014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "2e96ba684debc6206d03e649c33b7e00c7038d419c650384ad50a25aa582aea9" "basis" 2014 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/C