f the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=2{e}^{4x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-79774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "001e6c31292f1d3e7a2fe034dcf5f0bb4dc83c5860127b741446ebb7c34d7d54"} {"question" "q-79774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-79774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-79774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-65112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)=t$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}$ are solutions to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-2t{y}^{′}+2y=0$ for $t>0,$ what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-65112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)=t$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{t-{t}^{2}}{2}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-65112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "001e6c31292f1d3e7a2fe034dcf5f0bb4dc83c5860127b741446ebb7c34d7d54"} {"question" "q-65112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathaca