}_{2}{e}^{4t}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(t)={e}^{-t-1}=\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{e}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{e},{c}_{2}=0.$\n\n- The function $y(t)=7{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-4t}$ is **not** a solution of the given equation because ${e}^{-4t}$ is not a solution.\n\n- The function $y(t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{4}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=0,{c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-79799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "001e6c31292f1d3e7a2fe034dcf5f0bb4dc83c5860127b741446ebb7c34d7d54"} {"question" "q-79799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-79799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-79799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-131029" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{5}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-20y=0,$ what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-131029" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{5}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{5}+{c}_{2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=3,$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(x)=2{x}^{5}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}=\\frac{2{x}^{9}+3}{{x}^{4}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-131029" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "001e6c31292f1d3e7a2fe034dcf5f0bb4dc83c5860127b741446ebb7c34d7d54"} {"question" "q-131029" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d76