(x)=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{3}=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-131032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "001e6c31292f1d3e7a2fe034dcf5f0bb4dc83c5860127b741446ebb7c34d7d54"} {"question" "q-131032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-131032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2{e}^{-x}+{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-2x}}{x}"] ["math" "y=x{e}^{x}+{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "y={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-131032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-131036" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={e}^{x}+4{e}^{-x}$\n- $y(x)=5{e}^{-x}$\n- $y(x)=({e}^{-x}+1){e}^{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-131036" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{e}^{x}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}+4{e}^{-x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=1,{c}_{2}=4.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=5{e}^{-x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=0,{c}_{2}=5.$\n\n- The function $({e}^{-x}+1){e}^{x}=1+{e}^{x}$ is **not** a solution of the given equation because $1$ is not a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-131036" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "001e6c31292f1d3e7a2fe034dcf5f0bb4dc83c5860127b741446ebb7c34d7d54"} {"question" "q-131036" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-131036" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "q-131036" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee806d7632c7d246672232e2eee8608ec43343e0c39d611d09ae3e2af8801721"} {"question" "e-6865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^x$ are solutions to the equation $y''+y'-2y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{-2x}}{e^{x}}$\n\n- $y(x) = e^{-2x}\\cdot e^{x}$\n\n- $y(x) = e^{-2x} +e^{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/example-6865.json" "file_sha256" "2ed3a9b5e3b891cdf9cd6189378573a9f5722340a955be06e80d16c5ee4757e3"} {"question" "e-6865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous differential equation, then $y(x) = y_1(x) + y_2(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{-2x}}{e^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) = e^{-2x}\\cdot e^{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) =  e^{-2x} +e^{x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/example-6865.json" "file_sha256" "2ed3a9b5e3b891cdf9cd6189378573a9f5722340a955be06e80d16c5ee4757e3"} {"question" "e-6866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 4y = 0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = 2xe^{2x}$\n\n- $y(x) = 3e^{2x}$\n\n- $y(x) = -e^{2x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/example-6866.json" "file_sha256" "a38be365b90651bded3f2e5d6063022f872f7f3e1da4b33508600dc568bf632c"} {"question" "e-6866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the function $y_1(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x) = c y_1(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = 2xe^{2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x) = 3e^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=3.$\n\n- The function $y(x) = -e^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/example-6866.json" "file_sha256" "a38be365b90651bded3f2e5d6063022f872f7f3e1da4b33508600dc568bf632c"} {"question" "e-6578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{3x}$ are solutions to the equation $y'' - 2y - 3y = 0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$\n\n- $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$\n\n- $y(x) =- e^{x} + e^{3x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/example-6578.json" "file_sha256" "4444b7e1ca9f2cacf43f6ea50a2fef60fa7feef23c5524a2d96d3a4c0ce79f90"} {"question" "e-6578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c_1$ and $c_2$ are constants, then $y(x) = c_1 y_1(x) + c_2 y_2(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that $y_1(x) = e^{-x}$ and $y_2(x) =e^{3x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t) = c_1 e^{-x} + c_2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, $c_1 = 7$ and $c_2 = -6.$\n\n- The function $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation. The superposition principle only applies to linear combinations of known solutions. It does not extend to the divisions of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) = - e^{x} + e^{3x}$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation because $e^x$ is not a solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085055/example-6578.json" "file_sha256" "4444b7e1ca9f2cacf43f6ea50a2fef60fa7feef23c5524a2d96d3a4c0ce79f90"}] "usage" {"e-6865" [] "q-79730" [] "q-131032" [] "e-6866" [] "q-65112" [] "e-6578" [] "q-79724" [] "q-79774" [] "q-131014" []} "basis" 2888 "retractions" [[17592186152730 :question/problem "Given that $y_{1}(x) = \\sin x$ and $y_{2}(x) = \\cos x$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n1. $y(x) = (\\sin x\\cos x)^{2}$\n2. $y(x) = x\\sin x\\cos x$\n3. $y(x) = \\sin x + \\cos x$"] [17592186152732 :question/problem "Given that $y_{1}(x) = \\sin (2x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n1. $y(x) = \\sin^{2} (2x)$\n2. $y(x) =-\\sin (2x)$\n3. $y(x) = x^{2}\\sin (2x)$"] [17592186152559 :question/problem "Given that $y_{1}(t) = t$ and $y_{2}(t) = t^{2}$ are solutions to the equation $t^{2}y^{″} - 2ty^{′} + 2y = 0$ for $t > 0$, what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186150265 17592186150266 17592186150267 17592186150268 17592186150269 17592186151291 17592186151292 17592186151293 17592186151294 17592186151295 17592186151303 17592186151304 17592186151305 17592186151306 17592186151307 17592186343806 17592186343807 17592186343808 17592186343809 17592186343810 17592186343827 17592186343828 17592186343829 17592186343830 17592186343831 17592186343862 17592186343863 17592186343864 17592186343865 17592186343866] "immutable_fields" [[17592186150264 :answer-field/correct] [17592186150264 :answer-field/id] [17592186150264 :answer-field/key] [17592186150264 :answer-field/type] [17592186151290 :answer-field/correct] [17592186151290 :answer-field/id] [17592186151290 :answer-field/key] [17592186151290 :answer-field/type] [17592186151302 :answer-field/correct] [17592186151302 :answer-field/id] [17592186151302 :answer-field/key] [17592186151302 :a